    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Have you struggled with feeling driven by a motor like this since you were a child, or did it start recently?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, gosh, no, this isn't new at all. It's not something that just started with this family change recently, it's been my whole life. My mother used to joke that even back in elementary school, I was basically teaching the class from my seat. Well, I say \"from my seat,\" but I was really always out of my chair, talking to everyone, or cutting in before the teacher even finished giving the directions. \n\nWhich, being a teacher myself now, I completely understand how frustrating that is! But those old desks, you know, the ones where the wooden chair is attached to the desk with that thick metal pipe? I actually got my knee stuck between the pipe and the wood in the third grade because I was twisting around so much. The janitor—Mr. Abernathy, I think his name was—had to come up with a wrench to take the whole desk apart to get me out. He always smelled like peppermint and floor wax. I actually keep a bowl of peppermints on my desk now for my students because of him. Though I have to keep a close eye on them so they don't swallow them whole, or try to color with them, like Tommy. \n\nBut anyway, what was I... oh, right. Yeah, this motor thing. It was part of who I was long before adulthood. It’s always been there.",
