    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Aside from feeling constantly on the go, do you often find it hard to stay focused on tasks or details that require your sustained attention?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Focus? Um, I don't... I don't really think so? I mean, I can focus just fine when I need to. Like when I'm working with my students, I'm right there with them. We were doing this project on the solar system last week, and I spent hours cutting out these little foam planets. Foam is such a weird material, isn't it? It squeaks when you cut it with scissors. It's the exact same sound as when you rub two pickles together. Have you ever done that? My uncle showed me that trick at a family barbecue when I was maybe ten. He worked at a deli, so he always had these massive barrels of pickles. The garlic ones were the best. He used to say the secret was putting a whole clove of garlic in the jar, not just the minced stuff. I tried it once, but my wife said the fridge smelled like an Italian restaurant for a month. We ended up having to buy all these baking soda boxes to absorb the smell. You know, they say you're supposed to change those out every thirty days, but who actually remembers to do that? I usually just wait until the milk starts tasting funny. But, um... what were we talking about? Details? No, I don't think I have a problem with that. I'm pretty good at paying attention to things.",
